---
/**
 * ThemeManager Component
 * ======================
 * Handles dark/light mode with FOUC (Flash of Unstyled Content) prevention.
 *
 * ## Architecture Decision: Why Inline Scripts?
 *
 * We use `is:inline` scripts for theme detection because:
 * 1. **Bundled scripts are deferred** - They execute after the DOM is painted,
 *    which causes a visible flash when the theme doesn't match the default.
 * 2. **Inline scripts execute synchronously** - They run before first paint,
 *    allowing us to set the `data-theme` attribute before any styles are applied.
 *
 * ## How It Works:
 *
 * 1. `color-scheme: light dark` - Tells the browser to respect OS-level preferences
 *    for form controls, scrollbars, and other native elements.
 *
 * 2. `<meta name="color-scheme">` - Declares the supported schemes before the
 *    stylesheet loads (correct canvas colour, no white flash). It is a hint
 *    only: the `color-scheme` set on `<html>` by CSS / inline style wins, so
 *    the meta never drives the theme. Its content mirrors the pin state:
 *    - **Unpinned** (nothing saved in localStorage): `"light dark"` = follow
 *      the OS. This is also what the server renders, so it is the state when
 *      JavaScript never runs.
 *    - **Pinned** (`"light"` or `"dark"` saved): the literal saved theme.
 *    Both scripts below write it, and they MUST stay in step. The same goes
 *    for the `theme-color` pair (#141517 dark / #fafafa light), duplicated
 *    because this inline script cannot import `/toggle-theme.js`.
 *
 * 3. **Instant theme detection** (inline script):
 *    - Checks localStorage for saved preference (user has toggled before)
 *    - Falls back to `prefers-color-scheme` media query (OS preference)
 *    - Sets `data-theme` attribute on `<html>` BEFORE first paint
 *
 * 4. **Theme toggle button** (deferred external script):
 *    - Loaded from `/public/toggle-theme.js`
 *    - Two-state toggle: the target is the opposite of the RENDERED theme.
 *      If the target differs from the OS preference it is saved (pinned);
 *      if it equals the OS preference the saved value is removed, so the user
 *      can always return to "follow system".
 *    - While unpinned, OS changes (`matchMedia` "change") re-theme the page.
 *      A saved value is never removed or rewritten by an OS change, even when
 *      the OS later matches it, so a pinned theme stays pinned.
 *    - Other tabs sync through the `storage` event.
 *    - Re-applies everything on `astro:after-swap`: the ClientRouter resets
 *      the root attributes and head meta tags on every navigation.
 *
 * ## CSS Integration:
 *
 * Theme colors are design tokens compiled into `src/styles/tokens.generated.css`
 * (`:root` = light, `html[data-theme="dark"]` = dark). The same file carries a
 * `@media (prefers-color-scheme: dark) { html:not([data-theme]) }` block, so
 * the OS preference still applies when JavaScript is disabled.
 *
 * @see /public/toggle-theme.js - Theme toggle button logic
 * @see tokens/design-tokens.tokens.json - Theme color source of truth
 */
---

<!--
  Declares supported schemes before the stylesheet loads (canvas colour, no
  white flash). "light dark" = follow the OS; the inline script below and
  /toggle-theme.js pin it to "light" / "dark" to mirror the active theme.
  Without JS it stays "light dark", so the OS preference still applies.
-->
<meta name="color-scheme" content="light dark" />
<meta name="theme-color" content="#141517" />

<!-- Critical inline styles to prevent FOUC -->
<style is:inline>
  html {
    color-scheme: light dark;
  }
</style>

<!--
  GRACEFUL DEGRADATION: without JavaScript no data-theme attribute is set, and
  tokens.generated.css falls back to prefers-color-scheme for the palette
  (html:not([data-theme])). The only thing left to do here is hide the theme
  toggle, which cannot work without JS.
-->
<noscript>
  <style>
    #theme-btn {
      display: none !important;
    }
  </style>
</noscript>

<!--
  CRITICAL: This script MUST be inline to prevent FOUC.
  It runs synchronously before first paint.
  DO NOT convert to a bundled script.
-->
<script is:inline>
  (function () {
    const root = document.documentElement;
    const prefersDark = window.matchMedia(
      "(prefers-color-scheme: dark)"
    ).matches;

    // localStorage access throws in sandboxed iframes and some strict
    // privacy modes; treat that exactly like "no saved preference".
    let saved = null;
    try {
      saved = localStorage.getItem("theme");
    } catch {
      saved = null;
    }

    const activeTheme =
      saved === "dark" || saved === "light"
        ? saved
        : prefersDark
          ? "dark"
          : "light";

    // Apply every theme signal on every path: data-theme (CSS tokens and the
    // Tailwind dark: variant), the .dark class (third-party CSS), and
    // color-scheme (native controls and scrollbars).
    root.setAttribute("data-theme", activeTheme);
    root.classList.toggle("dark", activeTheme === "dark");
    root.style.colorScheme = activeTheme;
    // A saved override pins the meta; otherwise it stays "light dark"
    // (follow system). Mirrors applyTheme() in /toggle-theme.js.
    const pinned = saved === "dark" || saved === "light";
    const schemeMeta = document.querySelector('meta[name="color-scheme"]');
    if (schemeMeta) {
      schemeMeta.setAttribute("content", pinned ? activeTheme : "light dark");
    }
    window.initialTheme = activeTheme;

    // Update dynamic browser address bar theme-color. Keep these two values
    // identical to `themeColors` in /toggle-theme.js (this script runs before
    // <body> and CSS exist, so it cannot read computed styles).
    const meta = document.querySelector('meta[name="theme-color"]');
    if (meta) {
      meta.setAttribute(
        "content",
        activeTheme === "dark" ? "#141517" : "#fafafa"
      );
    }
  })();
</script>

<!-- Deferred script for the theme toggle button -->
<script is:inline defer src="/toggle-theme.js"></script>
